Z||000000||wxxo||03-10-2000||02:05 PM||||dumb question: what is the difference between the fieros?? like gt is pretty much the best but i got an 86 se. where do i stand? is an se better than a sport coup or are they the same thing...? well its a question thats been bugging me. i told you it was a stupid question haha!||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-10-2000||02:15 PM||||I too have an 86 SE V6 4spd. personal preferance really. There is no real better car. The SEs are lighter than the GTs by about 300lbs. the 88s have better brakes and suspension. The 84s although only in a 4 cylinder had the qicker tranny, with a 4:10 final drive. If your looking for monetarily better, uum FACTORY Blue or yellow cars, T-tops and of course the Coveted 15 Convertables produced just about all the 88s are worth more than the others. You have a great car let no-one tell you otherwise!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Deer_Slayer||03-10-2000||02:18 PM||||In 1986, the SE coupe got the aero styling which was on the previous 84 Indy and 85 GT.  The 86 coupe still retained the original fascias from the previous years.  As far as I know, the SE is suppose to be slightly better that the base coupe as far as options including the V-6 engine.  Hope that helped some.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-10-2000||02:39 PM||||The 84 thru 87 SE's are mechanically the same as the 85 to 87 GT's. I.E. brakes, suspension, trans (altought there are different gear ratios),etc. The only difference is 84 had 4cyl's 85 SE's the V-6 was an option and had the base coupe body. 85 GT's and 86/87 SE's had everything identical. 86/88 GT's had the fastback body and 15" wheels.<BR>Late 86 thru 88 the V-6 had a Getreg 5spd. Finally the 88's had updated/redesigned suspension and better brakes. The 88 Formula was a coupe body with GT suspesion and wheels. There was no SE offered in 88 (The SC was a base 4cyl coupe with the better suspension and 14" wheels from 86 to 88). 88's are very popular and with certain options (T-tops, leather seats, etc) are highly sought after.<BR>As for performance they are basically the same engines with minor differences. Example the 2.5L in 87 had a DIS (Distributorless ignition system). Handling for the 84 to 87 was the same for each suspension class. (base and performance) 88 redesigned suspension was better. for value the GT's and the 84 Indy Pace car are gennerally higher priced than the base, SC, and SE models. Hope this answers what you were looking for.  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||03-10-2000||02:41 PM||||Which Fieros are nicest will be a hotly-debated matter as long as there are still two Fieros running and two people to drive them, love them and argue about them.<P>But this seems to be the general pecking order among Fieroholics as to which are the nicest (assuming all cars are in excellent and equal condition):<P>1) 1988 GT<BR>2) 1986-87 GT<BR>3) 1988 Formula<BR>4) 1984 Indy Fiero<BR>5) 1985 GT<BR>6) 1986-87 SE V6<BR>7) 1985 SE V6 (non-aero body)<BR>8) 1986-87 SE 4<BR>9) 1988 Coupe<BR>10 (tie) 1984 SE; 1985-87 Coupe<BR>11) 1984 Coupe<P>Let me stress that I love them all, and that I don't think it's fair that the V6's get all the respect (except Indy's because they're rare).  But if you take this list to a Fiero show and watch the crowd, this is the order in which people seem to lust after the cars.<P>ZZZZIP!  Flame suit on.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||wxxo||03-10-2000||02:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChadMan:<BR><B>Which Fieros are nicest will be a hotly-debated matter as long as there are still two Fieros running and two people to drive them, love them and argue about them.<P>But this seems to be the general pecking order among Fieroholics as to which are the nicest (assuming all cars are in excellent and equal condition):<P>1) 1988 GT<BR>2) 1986-87 GT<BR>3) 1988 Formula<BR>4) 1984 Indy Fiero<BR>5) 1985 GT<BR>6) 1986-87 SE V6<BR>7) 1985 SE V6 (non-aero body)<BR>8) 1986-87 SE 4<BR>9) 1988 Coupe<BR>10 (tie) 1984 SE; 1985-87 Coupe<BR>11) 1984 Coupe<P>Let me stress that I love them all, and that I don't think it's fair that the V6's get all the respect (except Indy's because they're rare).  But if you take this list to a Fiero show and watch the crowd, this is the order in which people seem to lust after the cars.<P>ZZZZIP!  Flame suit on.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>yea i think that you are just asking for it with that reply but thanks for takin the heat to help someone out. thanks.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||03-10-2000||06:04 PM||||zip... i am now wearing my nomex suit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>so let me add that, now having drivin one, i would put the 88 formula ahead of the 86/87 GT's!  and i own a 86 GT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||ChadMan||03-10-2000||06:22 PM||||From the Fiero Spotter's Guide (have you ordered yours yet?), here's Mark Corbin's list of Fiero desirability, based on "collector's value."  <P>1) 1988 GT<BR>2) 1988 Formula<BR>3) 1986-87 GT<BR>4) Aero notchback V6 (1985 GT; 1986-87 SE)<BR>5) 1984 Indy Pace Car<BR>6) 1985 V6 SE (standard body)<BR>7) 1986-87 SE 4-cyl<BR>8) 1988 Coupe<BR>9) All others<P>Note that Corbin ranks the Formula ahead of the 86-87 GT, and believes the 86-87 SE and 85 GT are equally desirable.  He also believes the V6 aero notchbacks are more collectible than the Indy (don't tell Fiero Dave!).<P>This discussion is fun.  But if you truly love your Fiero - even if it's a beat-up 84 Econo Coupe with bad paint, ripped seats and 200K miles - then it's the nicest, and to hell with what everybody else thinks (especially me).  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||william_thorniii||03-10-2000||06:50 PM||||Chadman,<P>Your comment about the 84 ... is absolutely hilarious.  <P>The other problem is with idiots like me.  I first purchased an 87 EC then lusted after an Aero coupe.  Found an 85 GT, but due to sentimental reasons still love my 87 more.  Of course the gas mileage and stereo help as well  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Since I drive like a grandpa usually, the extra ponies don't make enough of a difference to be a selling point for me.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000009||william_thorniii||03-10-2000||06:59 PM||||OOOOOOPS! <P>I almost forgot the most desireable Fiero ever!  DGP IMSA body kit over any notchback with a fully Ryane-ized suspension underneath, Brembo 13" slotted rotors and 4 piston calipers and a twin-turbo Northstar pushing you out back.... Ahhhhhhh!  I feel much better now.  <P>When is the next session Doc?<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000010||Formula||03-10-2000||10:20 PM||||I drove an 86 GT and the only differnce it made suspension wise (86GT vs. 88Formula) was that the turning radius was completly different.  My Formula pulled quite a bit harder then the GT (both autos).<P>I think the 85GT in Black is the best looking fiero.||||reg||1
